[routines]
$0803 INIT: START A COMPILED PROGRAM. EVERY OBJECT FILE BEGINS WITH JSR INIT FOLLOWED BY A 17-BYTE HEADER: THE FIRST DATA ITEM, THE HIGHEST ADDRESS, THE COMMON BOUNDS (START-1, NUMERIC END, STRING END), THE LOCAL BOUNDS (THE SAME THREE), TWO BYTES EACH, AND THE COMMON FLAG (0 NONE OR DEFINE, $FF USE).|INIT POINTS THE & VECTOR AT THE PROGRAM (& RUNS IT AGAIN), SETS UP THE JMP VECTORS AT $58 (FLOAT THE INTEGER ACCUMULATOR) AND $5B (ONERR), FINDS THE LIBRARY'S OWN ADDRESS FROM THE OPERAND OF THE CALLER'S JSR, SO THE LIBRARY RUNS WHEREVER IT IS LOADED, PATCHES CHRGET FOR ONERR, COPIES THE HEADER TO PAGE ZERO AND CLEARS THE VARIABLES. IT RETURNS PAST THE HEADER.
$08A7 CLRCOM: CLEAR COMMON (REM! CLEAR COMMON). CLEAR: THE CLEAR STATEMENT. BOTH, AND THE END OF INIT, SHARE ONE ROUTINE WITH A IN THE ROLE OF A MODE: 1 CLEARS THE COMMON BLOCK, $FF THE LOCAL VARIABLES, 0 BOTH (INIT, NO COMMON OR DEFCOMMON).|NUMERIC VARIABLES BECOME ZERO; STRING VARIABLES POINT AT AN EMPTY STRING KEPT AT HIMEM-1. MODE 0 ALSO EMPTIES STRING SPACE. THE ROUTINE ENDS BY RESETTING THE STACK (RESETSTK).
$08DB HIMEM: (THE STATEMENT): THE NEW HIMEM ADDRESS IS IN FAC. STRING SPACE IS EMPTIED BELOW IT; VARIABLES ARE NOT CLEARED.
$0947 RESETSTK: EMPTY THE STACK, DROPPING EVERY PENDING GOSUB AND FOR, AND RESET THE DATA POINTER. THE BOTTOM OF THE NEW STACK IS A RETURN ADDRESS INTO NORET, SO A RETURN WITH NO GOSUB REPORTS RETURN WITHOUT GOSUB. PASS 0, 1 AND 2 OF THE COMPILER CALL THIS ROUTINE AFTER AN ERROR.
$0963 RESTORE: POINT THE DATA POINTER AT THE FIRST DATA ITEM.
$096C CHRPATCH: 15 BYTES THAT INIT COPIES OVER CHRGET AT $BA-$C8. THEY RUN THERE, SO THEY ARE SHOWN AS THEY RUN AT $BA.|FOR THE INTERPRETER NOTHING CHANGES: SPACES ARE SKIPPED AND THE ROM'S CHRGET GOES ON AT $F114. BUT WHEN THE ROM'S ERROR HANDLER, WITH ONERR ACTIVE, SETS THE TEXT POINTER TO $00BB (TXTPSV, SET BY INIT) AND CALLS CHRGOT, THE BYTE READ IS THE $20 OF THE PATCH'S OWN CMP, THE TEXT POINTER IS ON PAGE ZERO, AND THE PATCH JUMPS THROUGH THE ONERR VECTOR AT $5B INTO THE COMPILED HANDLER.
$097B POP (THE STATEMENT): DROP THE MOST RECENT GOSUB RETURN ADDRESS, PASSING OVER ANY FOR FRAMES ABOVE IT. ERROR RETURN WITHOUT GOSUB IF THERE IS NONE.
$09B1 FORUNWND: EVERY FOR FRAME STARTS WITH THE ADDRESS IN FORPOP ($09B0), WHICH IS ALSO A RETURN ADDRESS. WHEN A SUBROUTINE RETURNS WITH UNFINISHED LOOPS ON THE STACK, ITS RTS PULLS THAT ADDRESS AND COMES HERE, ONE PAST IT: THE REST OF THE FRAME IS DROPPED AND RTS IS TRIED AGAIN, SO RETURN UNWINDS THE LOOPS.
$09B9 NORET: RETURN WITHOUT GOSUB. RESETSTK LEAVES A RETURN ADDRESS TO HERE AT THE BOTTOM OF THE STACK.
$09BE FINDFOR: IS THERE A FOR FRAME ON THE STACK AT X? CARRY SET IF THE 16 BYTES ABOVE X START WITH THE FOR MARKER. SHARED BY NEXT (Y = 1), FOR (Y = 2) AND POP (Y = 3); Y SAYS WHERE TO GO ON.
$09DE FOR, THE START OF A LOOP. FOUR ENTRIES BY THE LOOP VARIABLE'S TYPE AND THE INITIAL VALUE'S: IFORINR INTEGER VARIABLE, REAL VALUE; IFORINIT BOTH INTEGER; FORINITI REAL VARIABLE, INTEGER VALUE; FORINIT BOTH REAL. THE CALL IS FOLLOWED BY THE VARIABLE'S ADDRESS.|THE VALUE IS STORED IN THE VARIABLE; A FRAME FOR THE SAME VARIABLE STILL ON THE STACK IS REMOVED (AS IN APPLESOFT); THE VARIABLE'S ADDRESS IS PUSHED AS THE FIRST PART OF THE NEW FRAME. THE LIMIT AND STEP ROUTINES PUSH THE REST.
$0A31 NEXT: THE CALL IS FOLLOWED BY THE VARIABLE'S ADDRESS, 0 FOR A BARE NEXT. FRAMES OF INNER LOOPS ARE DROPPED UNTIL THE VARIABLE'S FRAME IS FOUND (ERROR NEXT WITHOUT FOR IF NONE). THE STEP IS ADDED TO THE VARIABLE AND THE RESULT COMPARED WITH THE LIMIT IN THE DIRECTION OF THE STEP: THE LOOP GOES ON BY RETURNING TO THE LOOP ADDRESS KEPT IN THE FRAME, OR ENDS BY DROPPING THE FRAME AND RETURNING PAST THE CALL.|FOR FRAME, 16 BYTES FROM THE TOP: MARKER (FORPOP), LOOP ADDRESS-1, STEP (5 BYTES PACKED), LIMIT (5), VARIABLE ADDRESS. AN INTEGER LOOP HAS 0 IN THE 4TH BYTE, THE STEP IN BYTES 5-6, THE LOOP ADDRESS IN 8-9 AND THE LIMIT IN 10-11.
$0B11 ARRAY ELEMENTS. EVERY ARRAY HAS A ROUTINE IN THE PROGRAM: SEC (LOAD) OR CLC (ADDRESS) THEN JSR ARRREAL, ARRSTR OR ARRINT, FOLLOWED BY THE ARRAY'S DESCRIPTOR: ITS ADDRESS, THE NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS AND THE HIGHEST INDEX OF EACH, LAST DIMENSION FIRST.|THE FIRST SUBSCRIPT IS IN IACC; THE OTHERS WERE PUSHED BY PUSHSUB. EACH IS CHECKED AGAINST ITS HIGHEST INDEX (ERROR $6B, BAD SUBSCRIPT) AND THE OFFSET IS BUILT AS OFFSET*(HIGHEST+1)+SUBSCRIPT, THEN SCALED BY THE ELEMENT SIZE (5 REAL, 2 INTEGER OR STRING).|LOAD: THE ELEMENT GOES TO FAC, IACC OR THE STRING ACCUMULATOR. ADDRESS: THE ELEMENT'S ADDRESS GOES TO $DC/$DD AND ITS TYPE TO $D9 FOR ARRSTORE, WHICH STORES THE VALUE COMPUTED IN BETWEEN.
$0BC8 IMULT: INTEGER OPERATION 4: IACC = IACC * THE INTEGER AT (Y,A). SIGNS ARE TAKEN OUT (COUNTED IN $AB), THE MAGNITUDES MULTIPLIED BY SHIFT AND ADD, AND THE SIGN PUT BACK. ERROR $45 (OVERFLOW) IF THE PRODUCT DOES NOT FIT. MULSHARE IS THE SAME MULTIPLY WITHOUT SIGNS, USED BY THE ARRAY CODE.
$0C4E ARRLOAD: LOAD THE ELEMENT AT (Y,A) BY ITS TYPE (X: 0 REAL, 1 INTEGER, 2 STRING).
$0C6F ARRSTORE: STORE THE VALUE INTO THE ARRAY ELEMENT WHOSE ADDRESS AND TYPE THE ARRAY ROUTINE LEFT IN $DC/$DD AND $D9.
$0C7F DISPSTR: THE STRING VARIABLE DISPATCHER. A STRING VARIABLE'S ROUTINE IS SEC (OR CLC), LDA #, LDY #HIGH, LDA #LOW, JMP DISPSTR: CARRY SET LOADS THE VARIABLE'S POINTER INTO THE STRING ACCUMULATOR $77/$78, CARRY CLEAR STORES IT.
$0C9F INTEGER OPERATIONS 0 AND 1, ENTERED FROM DISPINT WITH (Y,A) = THE VARIABLE'S ADDRESS. INTEGERS ARE STORED HIGH BYTE FIRST. BOTH LEAVE THE FLAGS SET BY THE VALUE.
$0CC3 INEG: IACC = -IACC. IRSUB (INTEGER OPERATION 3): IACC = VARIABLE - IACC. ISUBT: IACC = IARG - IACC. IADD (OPERATION 2): IACC = IACC + VARIABLE. ERROR $45 ON OVERFLOW.
$0D06 DISPINT: THE INTEGER VARIABLE DISPATCHER. A NUMERIC VARIABLE'S ROUTINE LOADS X WITH THE OPERATION AND (Y,A) WITH THE VARIABLE'S ADDRESS AND JUMPS HERE.|0 LOAD IACC, 1 STORE IACC, 2 ADD, 3 VARIABLE MINUS IACC, 4 MULTIPLY, 5 COMPARE; 6-13 ARE THE REAL OPERATIONS 0-7 WITH THE VARIABLE USED AS A REAL: 6 LOAD FAC, 7 STORE FAC (ROUNDED, RANGE CHECKED), 8 ADD, 9 SUBTRACT, 10 MULTIPLY, 11 COMPARE, 12 LOAD ARG, 13 DIVIDE.
$0D35 ICOMP: INTEGER OPERATION 5: COMPARE IACC WITH THE VARIABLE. A = -1, 0 OR 1 AS FCOMP GIVES IT, FOR THE RELATIONS.
$0D5F ISTOREF: INTEGER OPERATION 7: STORE FAC IN THE INTEGER VARIABLE THROUGH APPLESOFT'S OWN LET, WHICH ROUNDS AND CHECKS THE RANGE.
$0DAE IREAL: THE REAL OPERATIONS ON AN INTEGER VARIABLE. X: 0 LOAD, 1 ADD, 2 MULTIPLY, 3 COMPARE, 4 LOAD ARG OR (WITH $64 SET) DIVIDE. FOR ALL BUT A LOAD THE CURRENT FAC IS MOVED TO ARG; THE VARIABLE IS LOADED INTO FAC AS A REAL AND THE OPERATION APPLIED.
$0DD9 HI-RES: DRAW AND XDRAW AT X,Y, HPLOT TO AND HPLOT. EACH HAS A REAL ENTRY (JSR AYINT) AND AN INTEGER ENTRY 3 BYTES LATER. X WAS SAVED IN $50/$51 BY SAVEHX AND THE SHAPE NUMBER IN $F0; Y IS THE ARGUMENT NOW. X MUST BE BELOW 280 AND Y BELOW 192 (ERROR $35, ILLEGAL QUANTITY).
$0E34 DRAW AND XDRAW: THE SHAPE NUMBER IS CHECKED AGAINST THE COUNT AT THE START OF THE SHAPE TABLE, THE SHAPE'S ADDRESS LOOKED UP, AND THE ROM DRAWS IT WITH THE CURRENT ROTATION.
$0E77 COLOR=. LIKE MOST STATEMENTS WITH ONE NUMBER, A REAL ENTRY (JSR AYINT) FALLS INTO THE INTEGER ENTRY, WHICH TAKES THE BYTE WITH CONINT AND GOES ON INTO THE ROM'S OWN CODE FOR THE STATEMENT.
$0E80 VALFN: VAL OF THE STRING ACCUMULATOR. THE BYTE AFTER THE STRING IS REPLACED BY 0 WHILE THE ROM'S FIN READS THE NUMBER, THEN PUT BACK.
$0EAA ASCFN: ASC OF THE STRING ACCUMULATOR INTO IACC (ERROR $35 FOR AN EMPTY STRING). LENFN: ITS LENGTH INTO IACC.
$0EC2 STRLITP AND STRLIT: A STRING LITERAL IN THE CODE: THE CALL IS FOLLOWED BY THE LITERAL'S ADDRESS. THE STRING ACCUMULATOR POINTS AT IT (IT IS NOT COPIED) AND THE CALL RETURNS PAST THE WORD; STRLITP ALSO PRINTS IT (AN INPUT PROMPT).
$0EEE PRSTR: PRINT THE STRING ACCUMULATOR.
$0F0B CLRCHAIN (REM! CLEAR CHAIN) AND FREFN (FRE): BOTH COLLECT GARBAGE. CLEAR CHAIN LEAVES THE LOCAL STRINGS OUT, SO ONLY THE COMMON STRINGS SURVIVE, PACKED AT THE TOP OF STRING SPACE, FOR THE NEXT PROGRAM; IT THEN GOES ON INTO CLEAR, WHICH CLEARS THE LOCAL VARIABLES.
$0F15 GARBAG: THE GARBAGE COLLECTOR. EACH ROUND FINDS THE HIGHEST STRING STILL IN USE THAT IS BELOW THE STRINGS ALREADY MOVED, COPIES IT TO THE TOP OF STRING SPACE AND REWRITES EVERY POINTER TO IT. THE POINTERS ARE THE COMMON STRINGS, THE LOCAL STRINGS AND THE TWO STRING ACCUMULATORS. LITERALS IN THE PROGRAM LIE BELOW STRING SPACE AND ARE LEFT ALONE.|FRE RETURNS THE FREE SPACE, FROM THE BOTTOM OF STRINGS DOWN TO THE HIGHEST ADDRESS USED BY THE PROGRAM. ERROR $4D, OUT OF MEMORY, WHEN A STRING DOES NOT FIT.
$109B OLDTEXT: 45 BYTES OF TEXT LEFT IN MEMORY BY THE ASSEMBLER: NEVER EXECUTED. THE BRANCH BEFORE THEM IS ALWAYS TAKEN.
$10CC GETSTR: GET A$: A NEW ONE-CHARACTER STRING FROM THE KEYBOARD.
$10DF CONCAT: THE PUSHED STRING FOLLOWED BY THE LOADED STRING, AS A NEW STRING (ERROR $B0, STRING TOO LONG, PAST 255). CONCATT SWAPS THE TWO FIRST.
$1135 PUSHSTR: COPY THE LOADED STRING TO THE PUSHED STRING.
$114B NEWSTR: MAKE ROOM FOR A NEW STRING OF {CHARAC} BYTES BELOW THE BOTTOM OF STRING SPACE, COLLECTING GARBAGE IF IT DOES NOT FIT, AND FILL IT FOR THE CALLER: A SAYS WHICH (0 CONCAT, 1 CHR$, 2 STR$, 3 LEFT$/MID$, 4 RIGHT$, 5 GET).
$1192 CHR$: A ONE-CHARACTER STRING.
$11A9 STR$: FOUT CONVERTS FAC TO TEXT AT $0100; THE TEXT BECOMES A NEW STRING.
$11C4 LEFT$, RIGHT$ AND MID$. THE STRING IS IN THE STRING ACCUMULATOR; THE COUNT (AND FOR MID$ THE START, PUSHED BY PUSHBYTE) ARE BYTES. A ZERO COUNT IS ERROR $35. WHEN THE RESULT IS THE WHOLE STRING NO NEW STRING IS MADE; AN EMPTY RESULT POINTS AT THE EMPTY STRING.
$1284 INPUT. INPSTR AND INPNUM READ THE FIRST ITEM (PRINTING "?"), THE P ENTRIES THE FIRST ITEM AFTER A PROMPT, THE N ENTRIES THE NEXT ITEMS. A NEW LINE IS READ WITH INLIN INTO $0200 WHEN NEEDED; $71 IS THE POSITION IN IT ($FF A NEW LINE, $FE USED UP).|STRINGS MAY BE QUOTED; ITEMS ARE SEPARATED BY COMMAS. A NUMBER IS CONVERTED BY FIN; A BAD ONE PRINTS ?REENTER AND THE WHOLE STATEMENT STARTS AGAIN AT THE ADDRESS INPBEG SAVED. CONTROL-C AS THE LINE'S FIRST CHARACTER STOPS THE PROGRAM.
$134B STOP: PRINT BREAK IN AND THE OBJECT ADDRESS OF THE STOP, THEN END.
$1363 PGMEND (END, AND NEW): PUT CHRGET BACK FROM THE ROM, MAKE THE PROGRAM SPACE AN EMPTY PROGRAM AND GO BACK TO THE INTERPRETER.
$1387 INPEND: THE END OF AN INPUT STATEMENT: ?EXTRA IGNORED IF THE LINE HAS MORE.
$1399 GETNUM: GET X: A DIGIT KEY GIVES ITS VALUE, ANY OTHER KEY 0.
$13A9 READ. THE DATA POINTER WALKS THE DATA ITEMS IN THE CODE: EACH IS A LENGTH BYTE AND ITS TEXT; $FF IS FOLLOWED BY THE ADDRESS OF THE NEXT DATA BLOCK (0 AFTER THE LAST: ERROR $2A, OUT OF DATA). A STRING ITEM IS USED IN PLACE; A NUMBER IS CONVERTED BY FIN (ERROR $10, SYNTAX, IF ANYTHING IS LEFT).
$1413 ERREXIT: EVERY LIBRARY ERROR ENDS HERE WITH THE ERROR NUMBER IN X. WITHOUT ONERR THE OBJECT ADDRESS OF THE CALL THAT FAILED BECOMES THE LINE NUMBER OF THE MESSAGE. X = $FF IS A CONTROL-C BREAK.
$1423 DEBUG: CALLED BEFORE EACH STATEMENT WITH THE RESUME/DEBUG OPTION. IT KEEPS THE STACK POINTER AND THE STATEMENT'S OBJECT ADDRESS, SO THAT AFTER AN ERROR RESUME CAN START THE STATEMENT AGAIN.
$143F STATEMENTS WITH ONE BYTE ARGUMENT: PR#, IN#, POKE (THE VALUE), WAIT (THE MASKS), TAB(, SPC(. THE REAL ENTRY CONVERTS FAC WITH AYINT; THE INTEGER ENTRY, 3 BYTES LATER, TAKES THE BYTE WITH CONINT (ERROR $35 PAST 255).
$148E COMMA: A COMMA IN PRINT: TO THE NEXT 16-COLUMN FIELD, OR A NEW LINE PAST COLUMN 24.
$14B0 ON ... GOTO AND ON ... GOSUB: THE CALL IS FOLLOWED BY THE NUMBER OF LINES AND THEIR ADDRESSES. AN INDEX OF 0 OR PAST THE LIST GOES ON AFTER THE LIST; OTHERWISE CONTROL GOES TO THE LINE, FOR GOSUB WITH A RETURN ADDRESS PAST THE LIST.
$14FD AND, OR AND THE RELATIONS. THE CODE BEFORE THE CALL HAS SET THE FLAGS: FOR AND/OR BY LOADING ONE OPERAND, FOR A RELATION BY COMPARING (A = -1, 0, 1). THE RESULT IN FAC IS 1 (TRUE) OR 0 (FALSE). THE ENTRIES SHARE CODE BY FALLING THROUGH ONE ANOTHER.
$152F STRING RELATIONS ON THE PUSHED STRING (LEFT) AND THE LOADED STRING (RIGHT). STRLT AND STRGE SWAP THEM AND USE THE CODE OF STRGT AND STRLE. EQUAL OR NOT EQUAL COMPARE LENGTHS FIRST.
$15BB STATEMENTS AND ARGUMENTS THAT KEEP A BYTE FOR LATER: ROT=, SCALE=, THE SHAPE NUMBER, THE THIRD WAIT ARGUMENT, A BYTE FOR SCRN( OR MID$, THE HI-RES X (16 BITS, SAVEHX), HCOLOR=, THE LO-RES X (SAVEX) AND THE OTHER END OF HLIN/VLIN (SAVEY), SPEED=.
$1627 INPBEG: THE START OF AN INPUT STATEMENT: KEEP ITS OBJECT ADDRESS FOR ?REENTER.
$1637 ONERR: THE CALL IS FOLLOWED BY THE HANDLER'S ADDRESS, WHICH GOES INTO THE ONERR VECTOR AT $5B.
$1656 RESUME: BACK TO THE STATEMENT THAT FAILED (ITS DEBUG CALL) WITH THE STACK AS IT WAS.
$165C PUSHSUB: PUSH A SUBSCRIPT UNDER THE RETURN ADDRESS FOR THE ARRAY ROUTINE.
$166E FLOATI: FAC = IACC. THE JMP AT $58 COMES HERE. IMOVE: IARG = IACC.
$167E LOOP LIMIT AND STEP, CALLED AFTER THE LOOP START. THEY PUT THE VALUES INTO THE FRAME UNDER THE CALLER'S RETURN ADDRESS; THE LIMIT ALSO PUSHES THE LOOP ADDRESS (THE RETURN ADDRESS OF THE STEP CALL) AND THE MARKER. INTEGER LOOPS: IFORLIM, IFORSTEP (IFORST1 = STEP 1); REAL LOOPS: FORLIM, FORSTEP (FORSTEP1 = STEP 1). THE R AND I ENTRIES CONVERT THE VALUE FIRST.
$16EB SCRN(: THE FIRST ARGUMENT WAS PUSHED WITH PUSHBYTE. POS: THE CURSOR COLUMN. THE RESULT GOES TO IACC.
$170A PLOT, HLIN, VLIN, PDL: THE LAST ARGUMENT; THE OTHERS WERE SAVED BEFORE. PDL GIVES ITS RESULT IN IACC.
$1738 STATEMENTS AND FUNCTIONS WITH AN ADDRESS: CALL, POKE AND WAIT (THE ADDRESS, SVADDR), PEEK. GETADR TAKES ANY VALUE FROM -65535 TO 65535. THE INTEGER ENTRY, 3 BYTES EARLIER, FLOATS IACC FIRST, SO -16384 IS A GOOD ADDRESS.
$175E PRINT A NUMBER: PRINTI FLOATS IACC FIRST.
$1764 DISPREAL: THE REAL VARIABLE DISPATCHER: X = OPERATION, (Y,A) = THE VARIABLE. 0 LOAD FAC (AND SET THE FLAGS), 1 STORE FAC, 2 ADD, 3 VARIABLE MINUS FAC, 4 MULTIPLY, 5 COMPARE, 6 LOAD ARG, 7 VARIABLE DIVIDED BY FAC.
$1796 COPYRT: THE COPYRIGHT NOTICE.
